{
  "id": "the-broken-alias",
  "name": "The Broken Alias",
  "category": "services",
  "difficulty": 3,
  "xp": 250,
  "level": 6,
  "description": "One name works, its alias does not.",
  "brief": "web.netquest.local resolves perfectly. www.netquest.local — the name everybody actually types — comes back NXDOMAIN.\n\nAn alias does not hold an address. It holds another name, which then has to be looked up in turn. If that second name does not exist, the whole chain collapses.\n\nUse dig to see the chain, and fix where it points.",
  "hints": [
    "Run 'dig www.netquest.local' on PC-01 and read the answer section.",
    "The CNAME points at a name. Does that name have an A record?",
    "Compare the CNAME's target with the A record that does exist.",
    "Edit the CNAME on DNS-01 so it points at web.netquest.local."
  ],
  "explanation": "A CNAME is an alias: it says 'ask about this other name instead'. The record itself was present and correct in form — it just pointed at a name nobody had ever created, so the second lookup failed and the client saw NXDOMAIN for the alias.\n\ndig is the right tool here because it shows the whole chain rather than just the final verdict, which is what makes the broken link visible.",
